<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Province</h1><p class="st-meta">1813 CE – 1817 CE · capital Montevideo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The self-governing Banda Oriental of José Artigas, which pioneered federalism and land reform before being overrun by Portugal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banda Oriental, east of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Uruguay</a> river, rose against Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1811 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Gervasio Artigas</a>. In 1813 a congress of the Eastern Province adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instructions-of-the-year-xiii">Instructions of the Year XIII</a>, demanding independence and a federal union with the other provinces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> rejected the Instructions and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1814. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">Artigas</a> broke with Buenos Aires and took Montevideo in 1815, governing through a cabildo while leading the wider <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-league">Federal League</a>. His Provisional Regulation of 1815 distributed confiscated land to poor settlers, indigenous people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, one of the first agrarian reforms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Portuguese troops from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> invaded in 1816 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in January 1817. The province was annexed as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cisplatina">Cisplatina</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">Artigas</a> fought on until 1820. Artigas is honoured as the founder of Uruguayan nationhood.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-province.jpg" alt="Eastern Province" width="320" height="338"><figcaption>Image: Diosverde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banda_Oriental_in_1816.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1813 CE – 1817 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Montevideo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Gervasio Artigas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provincia Oriental, Banda Oriental</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=eastern-province" aria-label="Eastern Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3255 3440 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eastern Province lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3575 3696L3575 3696"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3575 3696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Montevideo. <a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=eastern-province">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

